an in the living room, because you aren’t battling those cold surfaces. In summer, when the outdoor temperature is near or above 72°F, the exterior walls might actually be warmer than 72°F, reversing the effect and making the thermostat’s 72°F feel almost too warm. So your closet absolutely is a uniform 72°F haven. The fact that it exists and feels different proves that the rest of the house, where you actually live, is not. Your butter experiment was just missing the right thermometer placement: put it next to an exterior wall and you’d see the proof immediately. The house isn’t one temperature—it’s a patchwork of warmer and cooler surfaces, and your skin, like the butter, doesn’t lie.
